diff --git a/docs/containers/nixos.md b/docs/containers/nixos.md
index c7c7d59db..86a12fa9a 100644
--- a/docs/containers/nixos.md
+++ b/docs/containers/nixos.md
@@ -117,3 +117,33 @@ imports. To restore former latest-stable behavior for a modern generic import,
 restore the prior vpsAdminOS pin or choose an appropriate explicit module.
 The import change introduces no persisted state or data conversion. A separate
 NixOS release upgrade retains that release's normal rollback constraints.
+
+## Persistent allocation state
+
+The 25.11, 26.05, latest stable and unstable compatibility modules mount the
+selected `/var/lib/nixos` and `/var/log` directory binds before legacy user
+allocation. LXC mounts `/proc` before starting init, which causes NixOS to skip
+its early mount path. User allocation must see the saved maps before it runs;
+mounting them later leaves newly allocated IDs in `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/group`
+while exposing the original maps and file ownership on persistent storage.
+
+The compatibility step prepares absent backing directories and checks the
+mount before allowing allocation. Its source must be below an available mount
+separate from `/`; the supplied images use the host-mounted `/persistent`
+dataset. Custom root-backed sources are rejected, including layouts that
+previously activated successfully. Provision sources on separate backing
+storage before adopting the correction. Missing storage or a destination
+already mounted from a different source aborts activation before allocation.
+The step preserves existing content and metadata; absent directories receive
+root ownership and mode 0755. Custom sources requiring different ownership
+before allocation must be provisioned beforehand. Impermanence retains its
+normal later ownership and mode handling. Sysusers and userborn keep their
+existing activation paths.
+
+This preserves identities in healthy guests. It does not recover a guest whose
+UIDs or GIDs have already changed.
+Before rebuilding such a guest, compare and save its account IDs, allocation
+maps and persistent dataset. A rebuild can replace the saved maps with the
+current drifted IDs. Account or file ownership repair requires a separate
+recovery decision. Earlier generations still read the same map format, but a
+rollback restart can reintroduce the ordering defect.
